/*
:name: nested_tuple_return
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: nested_tuple_return
:pyrope_top: nested_tuple_return
:equiv_engine: cvc5
*/

// 2f-nested_type — a whole-tuple assignment from a call whose return nests two
// levels deep. A return is always a tuple, the one-field `io_data` wrapper
// auto-unwraps, and what is left binds by name at the same level -- ordinary
// assignment rules, no leaf flattening.
//
// This used to fail with "whole-tuple operation on `ctl` cannot be scalarized",
// and reading the result by nested path failed with "instance result has no
// output named 'ex.aa'". Neither was a detuple or epilogue limit: the callee was
// never SPLICED at all. `sub_instance_eligible` force-declined the splice for
// any callee with a two-dot output leaf, so the call stayed a Sub instance whose
// result handle holds no tuple value, no split and no bundle. The cliff sat
// exactly at the SECOND dot -- one-dot tuple returns already worked.
//
// The gate's stated reason ("the splice ABI materializes one level of output
// fields") stopped being true once the prologue started minting
// `inl<N>_<fully.dotted.leaf>` type_specs and the per-level field reads/writes
// were joined back to those leaves. Removing it is the whole fix.
//
// The golden is the FLAT hand-written equivalent, which is the real claim: the
// nested spelling must lower to exactly the same hardware as writing the fields
// out one by one.

pub comb unpack(bits:u4) -> (io_data:(ex:(aa:u1, bb:u1), wb:(cc:u1))) {
  io_data.ex.aa = bits#[0]
  io_data.ex.bb = bits#[1]
  io_data.wb.cc = bits#[2]
}

pub mod nested_tuple_return(clock:u1, bits:u4) -> (y:u1@[], z:u1@[]) {
  reg ctl:(ex:(aa:u1, bb:u1), wb:(cc:u1)) = nil
  ctl = unpack(bits=bits)
  y = ctl.ex.aa
  z = ctl.wb.cc
}
